 "rulebook": "World Athletics Competition and Technical Rules, 2024 edition: TR 26 (26.3-26.7 the uprights 4.00 to 4.04 m apart, the 4.00 m bar of at most 2 kg and 30 mm diameter on flat supports 4 cm wide and 6 cm long, 1 cm clear of the uprights), TR 27.1 (the take-off from one foot), TR 27.2 (failures: the bar does not remain on the supports; touching the ground or the landing area beyond the plane of the bar's nearer edge without first clearing the bar), TR 27.3 (a runway of at least 15 m), TR 27.10 (a landing area of at least 6 m x 4 m x 0.7 m), TR 25.17 (one minute for a trial)",
 "deviations": [
  "TR 27.1 (one foot) is judged by a number: in the last 0.05 s before the lift-off of the jump's flight only one foot touches the ground, and nothing else does",
  "TR 27.2(b) is judged geometrically: before the jump's flight nothing of the robot may touch the ground or the mat beyond the plane; the robot's pelvis and feet (the ankle joints) must pass the plane between the uprights and above the height of the bar's upper side; the bar is cleared when every part of the robot is beyond the plane",
  "TR 27.2(a): the bar is judged 2.0 s after the clearance and the landing; it is off its supports when its centre is more than 0.05 m below its resting height",
  "TR 27.10 (the landing area): the mat is a box with a soft, overdamped contact (time constant 0.08 s, full stiffness only 0.1 m deep), not a foam body; it is 0.1 m behind the bar's plane",
  "the bar is rigid (no sag); no wind; the athlete alone, one trial at one height, 1.0 m (FAMILIES.md: a school competition's opening height; the men's world record is 2.45 m)",
  "TR 27 does not define a jump; here its flight lasts at least 0.05 s and raises the pelvis at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off",
  "the bar is rigid and its supports are fixed, so unlike a real bar it would carry a body: touching it is allowed, as in the rules, but contact with it for more than 0.3 s in all is a failure (a real bar sags and falls under any weight); a pelvis or a foot that comes back over the plane has not passed it"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Do a high jump: approach the bar, take off from one foot, go over the bar between the uprights without knocking it off its supports, and come down on the landing mat.",
  "rules": "World Athletics rules for the high jump. The bar (4.0 m long, 0.03 m thick, 2.0 kg) rests with its upper side 1.0 m above the ground on two flat supports (0.04 m wide, 0.06 m long) that face each other from two uprights 4.02 m apart; pushed more than half a support's width forwards or backwards, or lifted, it falls. The bar lies across y in the plane x = 0; the plane the rules mean is that of its nearer edge, x = -0.03/2. Behind it lies the landing mat: 6.0 m wide, 4.0 m deep, 0.7 m high, soft, beginning 0.1 m behind that plane. The approach is free (any direction, any distance on the 15.0 m of level runway). A flight is a time of at least 0.05 s in which nothing of you touches anything but the bar. Your jump is the flight in which your pelvis crosses the bar's plane or which ends with your first contact beyond the plane. Its take-off must be from one foot: in the last 0.05 s before lift-off only one foot touches the ground and nothing else of you does; and in it your pelvis must rise at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off. Before your jump nothing of you may touch the ground or the mat beyond the plane. Your pelvis and both feet (the ankle joints) must cross the plane between the uprights and higher than the bar's upper side; one that comes back over the plane has not passed it and must pass it again. You have cleared the bar when every part of you is beyond the plane; from the landing until then nothing of you may touch the ground on the take-off side. You may touch the bar, for at most 0.3 s in all (it does not carry you), and you may land on any part of your body, on the mat or not. You succeed when you have cleared the bar and landed, and 2.0 s later the bar is still on its supports. A fall is not a failure by itself. You fail if the bar drops more than 0.05 m at any time; if you are in contact with the bar for more than 0.3 s in all; if you touch the ground or the mat beyond the plane without a jump; if the take-off is not from one foot; if the flight is not a jump; if your pelvis or a foot passes the plane outside the uprights or not higher than the bar; if you touch the ground on the take-off side after the landing before you have cleared the bar; or after 60 s (the time allowed for a trial).",
  "venue": "You stand on the runway facing the bar (+x), your pelvis 8 m before it, opposite its middle. The bar is red with white ends, the uprights grey, the mat blue; the runway is level and goes on 15 m before the bar and to both sides.",
  "difficulty": "The bar is at the height of the robot's hip joints: the whole 67 kg body has to be thrown over it from one leg. The robot has no running or jumping gait at all; its walking gait reaches 0.7 m/s."
 },
